      "Status": "preferred",
      "Explanation": "The phrase names a singular “road upward” repeatedly posed as beyond transmitted formulas. Panshan Baoji places it after both mind-is-Buddha and not-mind-not-Buddha and says the thousand sages do not transmit it, while students exhaust themselves like apes grasping reflections. An unnamed monk asks Dongyan Kexiu what the road is, and Dongyan answers by raising his robe collar. Ciming Chuyuan's variation says the thousand sages do not make it so. Other unnamed monks ask Xuedou Daorong, Bianlong, and Foyan Qingyuan about the road and receive sharply different replies: numerical nonsense, “beneath your feet,” and a refusal to frame it as upward or downward. The stable lexical item is the challenged road; the divergent answers prevent converting it into one explanatory teaching. Juelang Daosheng later distinguishes the road as separately honored after a three-sentence classification, adding an institutional deployment rather than another copy of Panshan’s line.",
      "Validation": "multi-source",
      "Note": "Seven exact witnesses from six distinct works. The duplicate Panshan parallel was retained only as attribution support outside curated depth and replaced by Juelang Daosheng’s distinct deployment.",
